From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 20 Sep 2026 12:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] filesystem: don't let the idle check block the main thread

CNfsConnection::CheckIfIdle and CSMB::CheckIfIdle run on the main thread, from
CApplication::ProcessSlow every 500ms. Both take the connection lock to close a
connection that has gone idle.

A directory listing holds that same lock across the mount and the readdir, and
a listing counts as no open connection - m_OpenConnections counts open files -
so while browsing a share that has stopped answering the idle check is entered
and waits behind the listing. The main thread then blocks for as long as the
server takes.

That reaches further than a plain freeze. A slow listing is already run on a
worker behind the busy dialog, which keeps the UI alive by pumping the render
loop on the main thread - and that pump calls ProcessSlow. So the thread the
dialog just freed blocks again within half a second, the spinner stops and the
cancel button stops being polled.

Closing an idle connection is an optimisation with no deadline, so take the
lock only if it is free and leave a busy round to the next one.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>

diff --git a/xbmc/filesystem/NFSFile.cpp b/xbmc/filesystem/NFSFile.cpp
index 965295e..89c3e1b 100644
--- a/xbmc/filesystem/NFSFile.cpp
+++ b/xbmc/filesystem/NFSFile.cpp
@@ -439,8 +439,11 @@ void CNfsConnection::CheckIfIdle()
    worst case scenario is that m_OpenConnections could read 0 and then changed to 1 if this happens it will enter the if which will lead to another check, which is locked.  */
   if (m_OpenConnections == 0 && m_pNfsContext != NULL)
   { /* I've set the the maximum IDLE time to be 1 min and 30 sec. */
-    std::unique_lock lock(*this);
-    if (m_OpenConnections == 0 /* check again - when locked */)
+    // A listing holds this lock across the mount and the readdir. Waiting for it here
+    // would block the main thread for the length of the server timeout, so skip a busy
+    // round - the deadline below still fires on a later one.
+    std::unique_lock lock(*this, std::try_to_lock);
+    if (lock.owns_lock() && m_OpenConnections == 0 /* check again - when locked */)
     {
       const auto now = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
 
diff --git a/xbmc/platform/posix/filesystem/SMBFile.cpp b/xbmc/platform/posix/filesystem/SMBFile.cpp
index 195c9da..a676aba 100644
--- a/xbmc/platform/posix/filesystem/SMBFile.cpp
+++ b/xbmc/platform/posix/filesystem/SMBFile.cpp
@@ -709,8 +709,11 @@ void CSMB::CheckIfIdle()
    worst case scenario is that m_OpenConnections could read 0 and then changed to 1 if this happens it will enter the if which will lead to another check, which is locked.  */
   if (m_OpenConnections == 0)
   { /* I've set the the maximum IDLE time to be 1 min and 30 sec. */
-    std::unique_lock lock(*this);
-    if (m_OpenConnections == 0 /* check again - when locked */ && m_context != NULL)
+    // A listing holds this lock across the connect and the readdir. Waiting for it here
+    // would block the main thread for the length of the server timeout, so skip a busy
+    // round - the countdown below simply resumes on a later one.
+    std::unique_lock lock(*this, std::try_to_lock);
+    if (lock.owns_lock() && m_OpenConnections == 0 /* check again - when locked */ && m_context != NULL)
     {
       if (m_IdleTimeout > 0)
       {
